# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the nightly run against a Qt built with
# TSan (test preset tsan-qt). Only races whose top frame on both sides is in
# Qt, each with a link to the Qt source that shows the bug
# (.claude/rules/threads.md). Always race_top: a plain race: entry matches any
# frame, and Qt calls roost's items synchronously from these functions.

# Qt 6.8, software scene graph, threaded render loop: on every expose the GUI
# thread writes QSGSoftwareRenderThread::exposedWindow with no lock, while the
# running render thread reads it in run().
# Write: https://github.com/qt/qtdeclarative/blob/6.8/src/quick/scenegraph/adaptations/software/qsgsoftwarethreadedrenderloop.cpp#L792
# Read:  https://github.com/qt/qtdeclarative/blob/6.8/src/quick/scenegraph/adaptations/software/qsgsoftwarethreadedrenderloop.cpp#L345-L352
race_top:QSGSoftwareThreadedRenderLoop::handleExposure

# Qt 6.12, macOS, QFileSystemWatcher (FSEvents engine): QFileSystemWatcher::addPaths
# in the GUI thread makes the engine's own strings (QFileInfo::canonicalFilePath,
# then the keys of watchingState.watchedDirectories) under the engine's lock, and
# the engine's libdispatch queue reads them in the FSEvents callback (processEvent
# -> checkDir, the hash lookups of the keys, QFile::encodeName). The hand-over
# goes through FSEvents and libdispatch, which TSan does not see, so each of the
# GCD thread's reads of the strings is reported against the plain writes that
# made them. Every address in the reports is a heap block allocated inside QtCore
# by addPaths; roost only calls the public addPaths from the GUI thread
# (PluginWatcher::Sync).
# Unlike the rule above this is `race:`, not `race_top:`: the top frame on the
# callback side varies with the inlining and the symbolizer (QString::normalized,
# QtPrivate::equalStrings, QHash buckets of DirInfo, ...), and one rule per
# top frame is a new nightly failure per frame. It cannot hide a race in roost
# code: the engine calls no code of roost, its signals to the GUI thread are
# queued, and only the engine's own frames are matched.
# Write: https://github.com/qt/qtbase/blob/6.12/src/corelib/io/qfilesystemwatcher_fsevents.mm#L316-L325
# Read:  https://github.com/qt/qtbase/blob/6.12/src/corelib/io/qfilesystemwatcher_fsevents.mm#L51-L58
race:QFseventsFileSystemWatcherEngine

# Qt 6.12, macOS: QThreadPrivate::start in two threads, an 8-byte write at the
# same address (palette_test: a finished QThreadPool thread, then roost's
# IoThread). The write is pthread_cleanup_push/pop, a macro of the SDK header
# that stores into the thread's own pthread_t, inlined into Qt's instrumented
# code: `__self->__cleanup_stack = &__handler` (usr/include/pthread/pthread.h
# of macOS SDK 26.5, lines 116-129; libpthread-539.100.4 include/pthread/pthread.h
# lines 116-123), the field at offset 8 of struct _opaque_pthread_t. libpthread
# puts the pthread_t at the top of the thread's own stack allocation
# (src/pthread.c lines 679-683 in _pthread_allocate) and hands the whole
# allocation back to the kernel when the thread ends (__bsdthread_terminate,
# src/pthread.c line 873); the next pthread_create can get the same address
# from mach_vm_allocate. Neither the unmap nor the new mapping is visible to
# TSan, so the new thread's push is reported against the old thread's pop. No
# roost data is involved: the only frame is Qt's, the address is the stack of a
# thread.
# Push: https://github.com/qt/qtbase/blob/v6.12.0/src/corelib/thread/qthread_unix.cpp#L442
# Pop:  https://github.com/qt/qtbase/blob/v6.12.0/src/corelib/thread/qthread_unix.cpp#L484
# Alloc: https://github.com/apple-oss-distributions/libpthread/blob/libpthread-539.100.4/src/pthread.c#L679-L690
race_top:QThreadPrivate::start
